1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,640
The next step in neutrophil function

2
00:00:03,640 --> 00:00:06,140
will be destruction of the phagocytosed material.

3
00:00:06,720 --> 00:00:08,840
Destruction of the phagocytosed material.

4
00:00:09,020 --> 00:00:12,080
So the neutrophils will phagocytose, form a phagosome,

5
00:00:12,160 --> 00:00:14,240
the phagosome will join with the lysosome,

6
00:00:14,580 --> 00:00:16,240
and that's called the phagolysosome,

7
00:00:16,340 --> 00:00:19,400
and then the phagolysosome will then be the area

8
00:00:19,400 --> 00:00:22,660
in which the material that was consumed is destroyed.

9
00:00:23,020 --> 00:00:24,340
So that's what we're talking about now,

10
00:00:24,400 --> 00:00:26,140
destruction of phagocytosed material.

11
00:00:26,720 --> 00:00:28,340
The destruction of phagocytosed material

12
00:00:28,340 --> 00:00:29,500
can occur in two ways.

13
00:00:29,500 --> 00:00:33,500
Remember that there is an oxygen-dependent mechanism,

14
00:00:33,820 --> 00:00:36,160
which is actually the best mechanism of killing,

15
00:00:36,620 --> 00:00:39,540
and then there is an oxygen-independent mechanism.

16
00:00:40,560 --> 00:00:41,660
And so these are the two ways,

17
00:00:42,040 --> 00:00:43,080
and as I've already said,

18
00:00:43,380 --> 00:00:45,360
the oxygen-dependent mechanism

19
00:00:45,360 --> 00:00:48,720
is actually the most effective way by which killing occurs.

20
00:00:50,100 --> 00:00:54,640
Now, this requires a little bit of biochemistry

21
00:00:54,640 --> 00:00:56,780
to understand this mechanism,

22
00:00:56,780 --> 00:00:59,480
and because there's going to be a disease process,

23
00:00:59,500 --> 00:01:01,160
that's important in this discussion,

24
00:01:01,580 --> 00:01:03,920
I do need to review some of that biochemistry with you.

25
00:01:04,160 --> 00:01:05,920
And I think the easiest way, again,

26
00:01:06,180 --> 00:01:08,220
would be for me to simply just create a whiteboard

27
00:01:08,220 --> 00:01:09,180
and explain this.

28
00:01:09,600 --> 00:01:12,540
So the mechanism by which killing occurs

29
00:01:12,540 --> 00:01:14,700
is going to be oxygen-dependent,

30
00:01:14,740 --> 00:01:16,420
so we're going to obviously start with oxygen,

31
00:01:16,900 --> 00:01:19,280
and one of the very first reactions that's going to occur

32
00:01:19,280 --> 00:01:23,520
is that oxygen is going to be converted to superoxide.

33
00:01:24,240 --> 00:01:25,460
All right, so superoxide.

34
00:01:25,820 --> 00:01:31,380
And the enzyme that does this is NADPH oxidase.

35
00:01:32,100 --> 00:01:33,480
So that's the first step.

36
00:01:33,940 --> 00:01:37,560
NADPH oxidase will take oxygen and convert it to superoxide.

37
00:01:37,560 --> 00:01:41,140
Very quickly, in an almost instantaneous reaction,

38
00:01:41,560 --> 00:01:43,980
superoxide will be taken by superoxide dismutase,

39
00:01:44,820 --> 00:01:46,960
which is an enzyme that we've already covered,

40
00:01:47,340 --> 00:01:51,140
and superoxide dismutase will convert this superoxide

41
00:01:51,140 --> 00:01:52,980
into hydrogen peroxide.

42
00:01:53,500 --> 00:01:57,160
And then hydrogen peroxide will be grabbed by myeloperoxidase,

43
00:01:57,900 --> 00:02:00,380
and will be, sorry, myeloperoxidase, MPO,

44
00:02:00,720 --> 00:02:02,400
and will be converted to bleach,

45
00:02:02,400 --> 00:02:04,460
which is basically HOCL.

46
00:02:05,400 --> 00:02:08,420
So an HOCL is the key molecule

47
00:02:08,420 --> 00:02:11,300
that is then going to destroy the organism.

48
00:02:11,920 --> 00:02:13,880
This biochemistry is all high yield.

49
00:02:14,200 --> 00:02:16,320
You have to know each and every one of these reactions,

50
00:02:16,720 --> 00:02:17,940
and you need to understand

51
00:02:17,940 --> 00:02:22,260
the step-by-step generation of this final product

52
00:02:22,260 --> 00:02:24,260
because there are going to be diseases

53
00:02:24,260 --> 00:02:27,220
that are going to apply to these different chemical reactions.

54
00:02:27,720 --> 00:02:29,340
So with that as a background,

55
00:02:29,540 --> 00:02:30,900
now let's go back and read together.

56
00:02:30,900 --> 00:02:32,900
So an O2-dependent killing,

57
00:02:33,240 --> 00:02:34,880
which is the most effective mechanism,

58
00:02:35,200 --> 00:02:36,140
what's going to happen?

59
00:02:38,040 --> 00:02:39,440
HOCL, i.e. bleach,

60
00:02:39,640 --> 00:02:42,100
is going to be generated by the oxidative burst

61
00:02:42,100 --> 00:02:43,720
in the phagolysosome,

62
00:02:43,960 --> 00:02:47,680
and it's this HOCL that's going to play the key role

63
00:02:47,680 --> 00:02:50,760
in destroying the phagocytosed microbe.

64
00:02:51,880 --> 00:02:52,860
All right, next slide.

65
00:02:53,320 --> 00:02:54,240
Now what's the mechanism?

66
00:02:54,340 --> 00:02:55,160
I've already gone through this,

67
00:02:55,220 --> 00:02:56,940
but let's read through it one more time.

68
00:02:56,940 --> 00:03:01,360
So oxygen is converted to superoxide by NADPH oxidase.

69
00:03:01,500 --> 00:03:03,500
That's also called the oxidative burst, by the way.

70
00:03:04,860 --> 00:03:07,920
Superoxide is quickly converted to hydrogen peroxide

71
00:03:07,920 --> 00:03:09,620
by superoxide dismutase,

72
00:03:10,000 --> 00:03:13,180
and then hydrogen peroxide is converted to HOCL,

73
00:03:13,340 --> 00:03:15,680
or bleach, by myeloperoxidase.

74
00:03:16,140 --> 00:03:17,500
So all of these are high yield.

75
00:03:17,640 --> 00:03:19,680
These enzymes are high yield, etc.

76
00:03:19,680 --> 00:03:22,400
Now, that's the normal,

77
00:03:22,780 --> 00:03:24,560
and there are a couple disease states

78
00:03:24,560 --> 00:03:26,460
in which this normal can be disrupted.

79
00:03:26,640 --> 00:03:27,540
And so the first of those

80
00:03:27,540 --> 00:03:29,780
is called chronic granulomatous disease.

81
00:03:30,340 --> 00:03:32,080
What happens in chronic granulomatous disease

82
00:03:32,080 --> 00:03:34,380
is that you get poor O2-dependent killing.

83
00:03:34,880 --> 00:03:36,980
Because O2-dependent killing is defective,

84
00:03:37,420 --> 00:03:40,280
the patient then gets chronic granulomas

85
00:03:40,280 --> 00:03:43,380
and gets regular infections and develops granulomas.

86
00:03:43,660 --> 00:03:45,600
So that's called chronic granulomatous disease.

87
00:03:46,020 --> 00:03:46,840
Now where is the defect?

88
00:03:46,840 --> 00:03:49,980
The defect is due to NADPH oxidase.

89
00:03:50,440 --> 00:03:52,760
Now this could be X-linked or autosomal recessive,

90
00:03:53,040 --> 00:03:56,720
but the key problem is that there is an NADPH oxidase defect.

91
00:03:57,740 --> 00:03:59,960
Now because there's an NADPH oxidase defect,

92
00:04:00,280 --> 00:04:02,820
the patients are going to have problems generating bleach,

93
00:04:02,860 --> 00:04:04,980
and because they have problems generating bleach,

94
00:04:05,100 --> 00:04:06,360
they're going to get infections.

95
00:04:07,400 --> 00:04:08,760
What's important here,

96
00:04:09,240 --> 00:04:10,560
and we're not done yet

97
00:04:10,560 --> 00:04:12,520
because there's a very important high yield principle,

98
00:04:12,980 --> 00:04:14,960
is that the patients get infections

99
00:04:14,960 --> 00:04:17,440
and they form granulomas with those infections.

100
00:04:18,000 --> 00:04:20,860
But the key type of infection that they're going to get

101
00:04:20,860 --> 00:04:23,760
are with organisms that are catalase positive.

102
00:04:24,800 --> 00:04:26,500
And there's a list of five organisms

103
00:04:26,500 --> 00:04:27,660
that are very high yield,

104
00:04:28,180 --> 00:04:29,380
but I'm going to just tell you

105
00:04:29,380 --> 00:04:31,480
what I think is the most high yield in a second.

106
00:04:31,840 --> 00:04:33,020
But I just first want to discuss

107
00:04:33,020 --> 00:04:34,780
this catalase positive idea

108
00:04:34,780 --> 00:04:36,920
because it does show up on examinations as well.

109
00:04:37,020 --> 00:04:39,080
So can we just go back

110
00:04:39,080 --> 00:04:40,280
to a whiteboard for a minute,

111
00:04:40,740 --> 00:04:42,880
and let me just go back to these reactions again.

112
00:04:42,880 --> 00:04:48,520
So you remember that oxygen is going to form into superoxide,

113
00:04:48,860 --> 00:04:54,960
and then superoxide is going to form into hydrogen peroxide,

114
00:04:55,400 --> 00:04:59,600
and then hydrogen peroxide is eventually going to form into bleach, right?

115
00:05:00,540 --> 00:05:06,080
And this was mediated by NADPH oxidase,

116
00:05:06,400 --> 00:05:10,220
and this was mediated by myeloperoxidase.

117
00:05:10,220 --> 00:05:12,480
Now, I just want to remind you that

118
00:05:12,480 --> 00:05:14,280
when you think about this reaction,

119
00:05:14,480 --> 00:05:15,680
what's really happening is that

120
00:05:15,680 --> 00:05:19,100
we're eventually generating hydrogen peroxide,

121
00:05:19,200 --> 00:05:21,420
and hydrogen peroxide is going to be converted to bleach.

122
00:05:22,040 --> 00:05:23,500
There are really two ways

123
00:05:23,500 --> 00:05:25,480
by which we can get hydrogen peroxide

124
00:05:25,480 --> 00:05:26,500
to convert it to bleach.

125
00:05:26,940 --> 00:05:29,200
One way would be to undergo this reaction

126
00:05:29,200 --> 00:05:30,000
that I've drawn here,

127
00:05:30,100 --> 00:05:32,500
where oxygen gets converted to superoxide,

128
00:05:32,680 --> 00:05:35,040
and superoxide produces hydrogen peroxide,

129
00:05:35,200 --> 00:05:37,740
and then this hydrogen peroxide becomes bleach.

130
00:05:37,740 --> 00:05:40,540
And that requires NADPH oxidase.

131
00:05:41,100 --> 00:05:42,420
Now, let's say that there's a patient

132
00:05:42,420 --> 00:05:45,000
who is deficient in NADPH oxidase, right?

133
00:05:45,060 --> 00:05:46,280
So this pathway is knocked out.

134
00:05:46,680 --> 00:05:49,280
There actually is another way by which this bleach,

135
00:05:49,560 --> 00:05:51,320
I'm sorry, by which this hydrogen peroxide

136
00:05:51,320 --> 00:05:52,960
can be generated to form the bleach.

137
00:05:53,440 --> 00:05:55,720
And that is that most bacteria

138
00:05:55,720 --> 00:06:00,540
naturally produce a little bit of hydrogen peroxide.

139
00:06:00,540 --> 00:06:03,160
So that for the vast majority of bacteria,

140
00:06:03,520 --> 00:06:06,300
we could simply, if we're lacking NADPH oxidase,

141
00:06:06,660 --> 00:06:08,680
we could simply take the hydrogen peroxide

142
00:06:08,680 --> 00:06:09,680
from this bacteria,

143
00:06:10,460 --> 00:06:12,340
grab the hydrogen peroxide from the bacteria,

144
00:06:12,600 --> 00:06:14,840
and then produce bleach that way.

145
00:06:15,360 --> 00:06:17,400
And so patients who have chronic granulomatous disease

146
00:06:17,400 --> 00:06:19,760
really don't have a problem with most organisms

147
00:06:19,760 --> 00:06:23,140
because most organisms are going to be able to,

148
00:06:23,200 --> 00:06:24,480
are going to have bleach,

149
00:06:24,640 --> 00:06:26,020
or sorry, have hydrogen peroxide,

150
00:06:26,120 --> 00:06:28,300
and that hydrogen peroxide can be converted to bleach.

151
00:06:28,300 --> 00:06:32,220
However, there are some bacteria that also produce catalase.

152
00:06:33,140 --> 00:06:35,760
And catalase destroys hydrogen peroxide.

153
00:06:36,140 --> 00:06:38,640
So that if a bacteria also produces catalase,

154
00:06:38,840 --> 00:06:40,880
this pathway now gets knocked out.

155
00:06:41,340 --> 00:06:43,080
And if this pathway gets knocked out,

156
00:06:43,160 --> 00:06:44,940
and this pathway is also knocked out,

157
00:06:45,200 --> 00:06:46,820
this can no longer be produced,

158
00:06:47,200 --> 00:06:49,420
and therefore bleach can no longer be produced.

159
00:06:49,940 --> 00:06:52,760
So that's why we only get infections

160
00:06:52,760 --> 00:06:55,400
with catalase-positive bacteria

161
00:06:55,400 --> 00:06:57,740
because it's with those catalase-positive bacteria

162
00:06:57,740 --> 00:07:01,260
that we no longer have any source of hydrogen peroxide.

163
00:07:01,740 --> 00:07:02,560
So I hope that's clear

164
00:07:02,560 --> 00:07:04,660
because that is actually very high yield.

165
00:07:05,280 --> 00:07:06,120
Now, in particular,

166
00:07:06,120 --> 00:07:07,540
there are five organisms

167
00:07:07,540 --> 00:07:09,300
that patients get infections with.

168
00:07:09,540 --> 00:07:10,260
Staph aureus,

169
00:07:10,840 --> 00:07:11,980
but everybody knows it,

170
00:07:12,080 --> 00:07:14,580
and it's a very, very classic bug,

171
00:07:15,380 --> 00:07:16,780
and it's not really the one

172
00:07:16,780 --> 00:07:18,940
that shows up on examinations most often.

173
00:07:19,360 --> 00:07:21,340
So staph aureus is a catalase-positive bug,

174
00:07:21,340 --> 00:07:24,640
and it is a common bug that would arise

175
00:07:24,640 --> 00:07:26,900
in a patient with chronic granulomatous disease.

176
00:07:27,300 --> 00:07:27,980
But I would tell you,

177
00:07:28,100 --> 00:07:28,740
in my own opinion,

178
00:07:29,000 --> 00:07:31,500
more important is Pseudomonas sepaceae.

179
00:07:31,980 --> 00:07:33,400
So Pseudomonas sepaceae

180
00:07:33,400 --> 00:07:36,320
is actually another important bug

181
00:07:36,320 --> 00:07:37,880
that is catalase-positive,

182
00:07:38,200 --> 00:07:40,060
and that can also result in infections.

183
00:07:40,440 --> 00:07:42,960
And because staph aureus is almost known

184
00:07:42,960 --> 00:07:44,120
by every medical student,

185
00:07:45,000 --> 00:07:47,780
examiners like to go after Pseudomonas sepaceae.

186
00:07:47,780 --> 00:07:51,300
So do not forget Pseudomonas sepaceae

187
00:07:51,300 --> 00:07:54,140
in the context of chronic granulomatous disease.

188
00:07:54,760 --> 00:07:56,360
I hope that I made that clear.

189
00:07:56,540 --> 00:07:58,260
But anyway, all this stuff is high yield,

190
00:07:58,500 --> 00:07:59,280
and it's a lot of fun

191
00:07:59,280 --> 00:08:00,940
because we get to tie in biochemistry

192
00:08:00,940 --> 00:08:03,020
and microbiology with pathology,

193
00:08:03,080 --> 00:08:05,040
and that's really what medicine is all about.

194
00:08:05,840 --> 00:08:07,620
The next important principle

195
00:08:07,620 --> 00:08:10,340
concerning chronic granulomatous disease

196
00:08:10,340 --> 00:08:13,180
is something called the nitro-blue tetrazoleum test,

197
00:08:13,340 --> 00:08:15,160
i.e. the NBT test.

198
00:08:15,580 --> 00:08:16,800
Now this is a test that's used

199
00:08:16,800 --> 00:08:19,120
to screen for chronic granulomatous disease,

200
00:08:19,480 --> 00:08:21,520
and what it does is it asks the question,

201
00:08:21,680 --> 00:08:23,340
this test asks the question,

202
00:08:23,920 --> 00:08:25,000
do we have the ability

203
00:08:25,000 --> 00:08:27,640
to convert oxygen to superoxide?

204
00:08:28,260 --> 00:08:30,920
If oxygen can be converted to superoxide,

205
00:08:31,420 --> 00:08:33,240
the superoxide will actually convert,

206
00:08:33,400 --> 00:08:35,200
will actually change the NBT,

207
00:08:35,540 --> 00:08:38,980
or this nitro-blue tetrazoleum, to blue.

208
00:08:39,400 --> 00:08:41,760
So why don't I make this super clear for you

209
00:08:41,760 --> 00:08:42,800
by drawing it in a picture.

210
00:08:43,160 --> 00:08:44,600
Again, you start with oxygen,

211
00:08:44,600 --> 00:08:47,880
and the oxygen gets converted to superoxide,

212
00:08:48,280 --> 00:08:49,220
as we've already stated,

213
00:08:49,480 --> 00:08:51,120
and then the superoxide gets converted

214
00:08:51,120 --> 00:08:53,320
to hydrogen peroxide,

215
00:08:53,800 --> 00:08:57,100
and the hydrogen peroxide gets converted

216
00:08:57,100 --> 00:09:01,520
to HOCl, right?

217
00:09:01,880 --> 00:09:06,460
Now, what does the nitro-blue tetrazoleum test ask?

218
00:09:06,520 --> 00:09:07,420
It asks the question,

219
00:09:07,580 --> 00:09:09,340
is this reaction intact?

220
00:09:09,340 --> 00:09:11,480
And if this reaction is intact,

221
00:09:11,620 --> 00:09:15,540
if you introduce the nitro-blue tetrazoleum

222
00:09:15,540 --> 00:09:19,180
into the test tube with the neutrophils,

223
00:09:19,540 --> 00:09:21,240
this will then turn blue.

224
00:09:22,620 --> 00:09:22,880
Okay?

225
00:09:22,980 --> 00:09:24,260
If the reaction is intact,

226
00:09:24,440 --> 00:09:25,380
the dye will turn blue.

227
00:09:26,040 --> 00:09:27,900
So if the reaction is not intact,

228
00:09:28,120 --> 00:09:31,500
the dye will actually remain colorless, right?

229
00:09:31,840 --> 00:09:33,860
And so patients who have chronic granulomatous disease

230
00:09:33,860 --> 00:09:36,740
are not able to produce this reaction,

231
00:09:36,740 --> 00:09:39,420
and therefore they will not be able to change the dye

232
00:09:39,420 --> 00:09:40,500
to its blue color,

233
00:09:40,820 --> 00:09:42,380
and the dye will remain colorless.

234
00:09:42,840 --> 00:09:46,940
So that's an important test to be familiar with.

235
00:09:47,300 --> 00:09:48,900
Now, there is another disease

236
00:09:48,900 --> 00:09:51,040
that can also affect this pathway,

237
00:09:51,480 --> 00:09:54,740
and that is the fact that MPO converts,

238
00:09:55,420 --> 00:09:58,740
remember that MPO converts hydrogen peroxide to HOCl.

239
00:09:59,700 --> 00:10:01,460
MPO can be defective as well.

240
00:10:01,940 --> 00:10:03,500
And if MPO is defective,

241
00:10:03,500 --> 00:10:05,060
that's called an MPO deficiency.

242
00:10:05,660 --> 00:10:08,120
And again, patients will not be able to produce bleach.

243
00:10:08,780 --> 00:10:11,840
So in MPO or myeloperoxidase deficiency,

244
00:10:12,340 --> 00:10:14,860
the interesting thing is that the vast majority of patients

245
00:10:14,860 --> 00:10:16,200
are actually asymptomatic.

246
00:10:16,380 --> 00:10:17,300
So in real life,

247
00:10:17,380 --> 00:10:18,900
if you were to ever see such a patient,

248
00:10:19,020 --> 00:10:19,760
which is very rare,

249
00:10:20,160 --> 00:10:21,500
but if you were to ever see such a patient,

250
00:10:21,600 --> 00:10:23,100
they would be asymptomatic most likely.

251
00:10:23,500 --> 00:10:24,760
However, on examinations,

252
00:10:25,200 --> 00:10:27,500
they get an increased risk for candida infections.

253
00:10:27,700 --> 00:10:28,940
And I guess in real life as well,

254
00:10:29,000 --> 00:10:30,460
if they're ever going to have symptoms,

255
00:10:30,860 --> 00:10:32,560
it's going to be candida infections,

256
00:10:32,560 --> 00:10:34,820
although usually these patients don't have symptoms.

257
00:10:34,940 --> 00:10:35,780
But on board exams,

258
00:10:36,120 --> 00:10:38,280
they usually have an increased risk for candida infections.

259
00:10:38,820 --> 00:10:40,980
I'm going to talk about this in a slide in a minute.

260
00:10:41,320 --> 00:10:42,520
However, what I want to highlight here,

261
00:10:42,580 --> 00:10:44,420
since I took all the pain to draw this picture,

262
00:10:44,800 --> 00:10:47,420
is that the NBT test will be normal.

263
00:10:47,740 --> 00:10:48,960
Because in these patients,

264
00:10:49,140 --> 00:10:50,440
in myeloperoxidase deficiency,

265
00:10:50,780 --> 00:10:52,020
this reaction is fine.

266
00:10:52,460 --> 00:10:54,340
The problem is this reaction down here.

267
00:10:54,660 --> 00:10:56,440
So the NBT test will turn blue,

268
00:10:56,840 --> 00:10:59,940
but the patients will be lacking myeloperoxidase instead.

269
00:10:59,940 --> 00:11:01,760
Okay, so with that,

270
00:11:02,100 --> 00:11:06,160
let's actually talk briefly about this slide,

271
00:11:06,320 --> 00:11:08,160
which is the next slide,

272
00:11:08,260 --> 00:11:09,520
which is the MPO deficiency.

273
00:11:10,160 --> 00:11:12,300
So MPO deficiency results in what?

274
00:11:12,360 --> 00:11:15,520
It's a defective conversion of H2O2 to HOCL.

275
00:11:15,700 --> 00:11:17,240
I've already explained that to you.

276
00:11:17,700 --> 00:11:19,620
And most patients are asymptomatic.

277
00:11:20,020 --> 00:11:22,440
However, if there ever is a problem,

278
00:11:22,580 --> 00:11:24,400
the problem is usually an increased risk

279
00:11:24,400 --> 00:11:25,660
for candida infections.

280
00:11:25,660 --> 00:11:29,140
And high-yield NBT test is normal.

281
00:11:29,580 --> 00:11:30,820
So it is important to be familiar

282
00:11:30,820 --> 00:11:33,620
with myeloperoxidase deficiency.

283
00:11:34,160 --> 00:11:35,020
Now let's go forward.

284
00:11:35,500 --> 00:11:37,280
The second way by which neutrophils can kill

285
00:11:37,280 --> 00:11:39,360
is called O2-independent killing.

286
00:11:39,720 --> 00:11:40,580
It's less effective,

287
00:11:40,820 --> 00:11:41,980
and it occurs via enzymes

288
00:11:41,980 --> 00:11:43,600
that are present within the leukocyte,

289
00:11:43,920 --> 00:11:46,680
usually within the secondary granules of the leukocyte.

290
00:11:47,460 --> 00:11:48,280
So for example,

291
00:11:48,800 --> 00:11:50,000
lysozyme is an enzyme

292
00:11:50,000 --> 00:11:51,920
that's useful for destroying microbes.

293
00:11:52,360 --> 00:11:53,480
Major basic protein.

294
00:11:53,480 --> 00:11:55,640
This is major basic protein here.

295
00:11:55,800 --> 00:11:57,480
It is an enzyme present in eosinophils,

296
00:11:58,140 --> 00:12:00,660
which is useful for destroying microbes as well.

297
00:12:01,000 --> 00:12:02,580
And this is a little less effective

298
00:12:02,580 --> 00:12:04,680
than oxygen-dependent killing.

299
00:12:05,320 --> 00:12:07,820
The final stage of neutrophil arrival

300
00:12:07,820 --> 00:12:08,760
is called resolution.

301
00:12:09,460 --> 00:12:11,040
So once the infection is destroyed,

302
00:12:11,160 --> 00:12:12,160
within 24 hours,

303
00:12:12,240 --> 00:12:13,260
the neutrophils will disappear.

304
00:12:13,920 --> 00:12:15,200
Now, it's important for you to know

305
00:12:15,200 --> 00:12:15,480
for examinations,

306
00:12:16,500 --> 00:12:17,180
they like you to know

307
00:12:17,180 --> 00:12:18,480
how neutrophils disappear.

308
00:12:19,120 --> 00:12:20,040
And what happens is

309
00:12:20,040 --> 00:12:22,380
the neutrophils die within the tissue,

310
00:12:22,380 --> 00:12:24,460
and they die via apoptosis.

311
00:12:24,940 --> 00:12:26,120
So all neutrophils,

312
00:12:26,220 --> 00:12:27,200
once they get into tissue,

313
00:12:27,320 --> 00:12:28,580
will die within the tissue,

314
00:12:28,900 --> 00:12:30,440
and they'll die via apoptosis.

315
00:12:30,840 --> 00:12:31,980
And recall that pus

316
00:12:31,980 --> 00:12:35,820
is basically dead neutrophils within fluid.

317
00:12:36,220 --> 00:12:38,900
And that's sort of the final resolution,

318
00:12:39,080 --> 00:12:40,140
which is you have to know

319
00:12:40,140 --> 00:12:41,560
that it's very high yield.

320
00:12:42,040 --> 00:12:44,420
Neutrophils undergo apoptosis,

321
00:12:44,620 --> 00:12:45,920
and that's the way by which

322
00:12:45,920 --> 00:12:48,100
they resolve themselves within the tissue.

323
00:12:48,100 --> 00:12:50,340
Now, once the neutrophils

324
00:12:50,340 --> 00:12:53,540
are sort of done their job,

325
00:12:53,620 --> 00:12:54,600
the next phase will be

326
00:12:54,600 --> 00:12:55,580
to bring in the macrophages.

327
00:12:55,920 --> 00:12:57,000
Can you remember I told you

328
00:12:57,000 --> 00:12:57,940
that there were three phases

329
00:12:57,940 --> 00:12:58,660
of acute inflammation?

330
00:12:58,960 --> 00:12:59,960
There's a fluid phase,

331
00:13:00,140 --> 00:13:01,560
which occurs immediately.

332
00:13:01,880 --> 00:13:02,980
There's a neutrophil phase,

333
00:13:03,080 --> 00:13:05,220
which peaks at 24 hours

334
00:13:05,220 --> 00:13:06,200
or a day into inflammation.

335
00:13:06,760 --> 00:13:08,000
And then there's a final phase,

336
00:13:08,240 --> 00:13:09,100
and that final phase

337
00:13:09,100 --> 00:13:10,220
is the macrophage phase.

338
00:13:10,680 --> 00:13:11,380
And so this is now

339
00:13:11,380 --> 00:13:12,620
what we want to briefly touch on,

340
00:13:12,660 --> 00:13:13,560
the macrophage phase.

341
00:13:13,920 --> 00:13:15,160
And so the macrophages peak

342
00:13:15,160 --> 00:13:16,040
two to three days

343
00:13:16,040 --> 00:13:17,440
after inflammation begins.

344
00:13:18,260 --> 00:13:20,320
They occur after the neutrophils arrive,

345
00:13:20,760 --> 00:13:22,020
and they're derived

346
00:13:22,020 --> 00:13:23,220
from monocytes in the blood.

347
00:13:23,340 --> 00:13:24,500
So you've got these monocytes

348
00:13:24,500 --> 00:13:25,620
floating around in the blood,

349
00:13:25,980 --> 00:13:26,880
and it's the monocytes

350
00:13:26,880 --> 00:13:28,060
that eventually get into tissue,

351
00:13:28,140 --> 00:13:29,080
and when they get into tissue,

352
00:13:29,080 --> 00:13:30,220
we call them macrophages.

353
00:13:30,760 --> 00:13:32,140
Now, we should just be thankful

354
00:13:32,140 --> 00:13:34,200
that the macrophages,

355
00:13:34,400 --> 00:13:35,280
i.e. the monocytes,

356
00:13:35,380 --> 00:13:36,440
they come into the tissue

357
00:13:36,440 --> 00:13:37,860
the exact same way

358
00:13:37,860 --> 00:13:38,780
that the neutrophils do.

359
00:13:39,100 --> 00:13:40,340
So they use margination,

360
00:13:40,580 --> 00:13:41,320
they use rolling,

361
00:13:41,640 --> 00:13:42,460
they use adhesion,

362
00:13:42,680 --> 00:13:43,680
they use transmigration.

363
00:13:44,000 --> 00:13:44,520
So guess what?

364
00:13:44,520 --> 00:13:45,760
That makes their life really easy,

365
00:13:46,120 --> 00:13:47,580
because now we don't have to repeat

366
00:13:47,580 --> 00:13:48,480
all of these steps.

367
00:13:48,560 --> 00:13:49,720
We're already familiar with them.

368
00:13:50,380 --> 00:13:52,300
Once the macrophages

369
00:13:52,300 --> 00:13:53,180
get into the tissue,

370
00:13:53,540 --> 00:13:54,580
they actually ingest

371
00:13:54,580 --> 00:13:55,980
via phagocytosis,

372
00:13:56,420 --> 00:13:57,720
and then they destroy

373
00:13:57,720 --> 00:13:59,100
the phagocytosed material

374
00:13:59,100 --> 00:14:00,220
using enzymes

375
00:14:00,220 --> 00:14:01,440
in the secondary granules.

376
00:14:01,980 --> 00:14:03,000
For the most part,

377
00:14:03,140 --> 00:14:04,080
neutrophil, sorry,

378
00:14:04,180 --> 00:14:04,980
for the most part,

379
00:14:05,080 --> 00:14:07,060
macrophages don't really do

380
00:14:07,060 --> 00:14:08,800
a lot of oxygen-dependent killing.

381
00:14:09,160 --> 00:14:10,620
Their primary mechanism

382
00:14:10,620 --> 00:14:13,900
is oxygen-independent

383
00:14:13,900 --> 00:14:15,000
using enzymes

384
00:14:15,000 --> 00:14:16,200
in the secondary granules,

385
00:14:16,400 --> 00:14:16,880
and I would say

386
00:14:16,880 --> 00:14:17,880
one of the key enzymes

387
00:14:17,880 --> 00:14:18,940
is lysozyme.

388
00:14:20,560 --> 00:14:22,200
So we should be familiar

389
00:14:22,200 --> 00:14:22,660
with the fact

390
00:14:22,660 --> 00:14:23,420
that lysozyme

391
00:14:23,420 --> 00:14:24,580
in the secondary granules

392
00:14:24,580 --> 00:14:25,780
are very useful

393
00:14:25,780 --> 00:14:26,820
for the macrophages

394
00:14:26,820 --> 00:14:29,820
to destroy phagocytosed material

395
00:14:29,820 --> 00:14:30,660
or to kill off

396
00:14:30,660 --> 00:14:31,720
phagocytosed material.

397
00:14:32,120 --> 00:14:33,300
The final point

398
00:14:33,300 --> 00:14:34,900
to close off this section

399
00:14:34,900 --> 00:14:36,480
is that the macrophages,

400
00:14:36,900 --> 00:14:37,740
in my mind,

401
00:14:37,840 --> 00:14:38,420
sort of the way

402
00:14:38,420 --> 00:14:39,400
I think about macrophages

403
00:14:39,400 --> 00:14:40,520
is that they are managers.

404
00:14:40,940 --> 00:14:41,700
They then manage

405
00:14:41,700 --> 00:14:42,860
the next step

406
00:14:42,860 --> 00:14:44,440
of the acute inflammatory process.

407
00:14:44,920 --> 00:14:45,780
And so when the macrophages

408
00:14:45,780 --> 00:14:46,220
come in,

409
00:14:46,260 --> 00:14:47,100
they sort of look around

410
00:14:47,100 --> 00:14:47,660
their managers

411
00:14:47,660 --> 00:14:48,320
and they say,

412
00:14:48,720 --> 00:14:50,480
hmm, has a good job been done?

413
00:14:50,600 --> 00:14:51,220
Does something more

414
00:14:51,220 --> 00:14:51,800
need to be done?

415
00:14:51,880 --> 00:14:52,560
Do we need to call

416
00:14:52,560 --> 00:14:53,340
in somebody else?

417
00:14:53,720 --> 00:14:54,480
So if they come

418
00:14:54,480 --> 00:14:55,100
into the tissue

419
00:14:55,100 --> 00:14:56,060
and they see that,

420
00:14:56,440 --> 00:14:57,340
well, excellent job,

421
00:14:57,420 --> 00:14:57,920
the neutrophils

422
00:14:57,920 --> 00:14:59,020
have really killed off everything

423
00:14:59,020 --> 00:15:00,000
and it's now time

424
00:15:00,000 --> 00:15:01,280
to begin the next step,

425
00:15:01,600 --> 00:15:02,900
they will then induce

426
00:15:02,900 --> 00:15:04,460
resolution and healing.

427
00:15:05,160 --> 00:15:05,920
So they'll still

428
00:15:05,920 --> 00:15:06,660
kind of look around

429
00:15:06,660 --> 00:15:06,960
and say,

430
00:15:07,020 --> 00:15:07,580
hmm, okay,

431
00:15:07,580 --> 00:15:08,440
these bugs are dead,

432
00:15:08,520 --> 00:15:09,200
these bugs are dead,

433
00:15:09,280 --> 00:15:09,980
these bugs are dead.

434
00:15:10,140 --> 00:15:11,320
Neutrophils did a great job.

435
00:15:11,580 --> 00:15:13,120
Let's call in healing

436
00:15:13,120 --> 00:15:14,080
because it's now time

437
00:15:14,080 --> 00:15:15,060
to fix the tissue.

438
00:15:15,560 --> 00:15:16,340
And the way by which

439
00:15:16,340 --> 00:15:16,940
they do that

440
00:15:16,940 --> 00:15:19,160
is partially by secreting

441
00:15:19,160 --> 00:15:20,920
IL-10 and TGF-beta.

442
00:15:21,320 --> 00:15:22,080
Now the key thing

443
00:15:22,080 --> 00:15:23,520
about IL-10 and TGF-beta,

444
00:15:23,680 --> 00:15:24,680
these are both things

445
00:15:24,680 --> 00:15:25,460
that shut down

446
00:15:25,460 --> 00:15:26,600
the inflammatory process.

447
00:15:27,100 --> 00:15:28,120
So I think it's important

448
00:15:28,120 --> 00:15:28,520
to remember

449
00:15:28,520 --> 00:15:30,980
that IL-10 and TGF-beta,

450
00:15:31,280 --> 00:15:31,820
they're kind of

451
00:15:31,820 --> 00:15:33,080
anti-inflammatory.

452
00:15:33,260 --> 00:15:34,280
They sort of shut down

453
00:15:34,280 --> 00:15:35,660
the inflammatory process

454
00:15:35,660 --> 00:15:37,500
and then they also allow

455
00:15:37,500 --> 00:15:38,780
for the eventual

456
00:15:38,780 --> 00:15:41,220
calling in of healing.

457
00:15:41,360 --> 00:15:41,900
And we're going to talk

458
00:15:41,900 --> 00:15:42,680
about wound healing

459
00:15:42,680 --> 00:15:43,580
a little later

460
00:15:43,580 --> 00:15:44,760
in this chapter.

461
00:15:45,680 --> 00:15:46,660
Another possibility

462
00:15:46,660 --> 00:15:47,980
is that the neutrophils

463
00:15:47,980 --> 00:15:48,640
can come in

464
00:15:48,640 --> 00:15:49,640
and they could say,

465
00:15:49,780 --> 00:15:50,140
uh-oh,

466
00:15:50,460 --> 00:15:51,040
we've got to do

467
00:15:51,040 --> 00:15:51,900
a lot more work.

468
00:15:52,240 --> 00:15:53,020
They may come in

469
00:15:53,020 --> 00:15:53,880
two to three days later

470
00:15:53,880 --> 00:15:54,300
and say,

471
00:15:54,460 --> 00:15:55,400
neutrophils look like

472
00:15:55,400 --> 00:15:56,180
they need some help.

473
00:15:56,360 --> 00:15:57,180
So they can actually

474
00:15:57,180 --> 00:15:59,060
call in additional neutrophils

475
00:15:59,060 --> 00:16:00,000
allowing for acute,

476
00:16:00,380 --> 00:16:01,740
continued acute inflammation.

477
00:16:02,360 --> 00:16:03,600
A couple important points here

478
00:16:03,600 --> 00:16:04,020
which become

479
00:16:07,500 --> 00:16:08,160
is the mechanism

480
00:16:08,160 --> 00:16:09,180
by which the neutrophils

481
00:16:09,180 --> 00:16:09,780
call in,

482
00:16:09,940 --> 00:16:10,220
sorry,

483
00:16:10,340 --> 00:16:11,060
is the mechanism

484
00:16:11,060 --> 00:16:12,640
by which the macrophages

485
00:16:12,640 --> 00:16:14,220
call in the neutrophils.

486
00:16:14,560 --> 00:16:14,820
Again,

487
00:16:14,880 --> 00:16:15,760
I'm going to repeat this

488
00:16:15,760 --> 00:16:16,460
because it's high yield.

489
00:16:16,880 --> 00:16:17,600
IL-8

490
00:16:17,600 --> 00:16:19,560
is the cytokine

491
00:16:19,560 --> 00:16:21,760
produced by macrophages

492
00:16:21,760 --> 00:16:23,220
that call in the neutrophil.

493
00:16:23,660 --> 00:16:25,080
Now when additional neutrophils

494
00:16:25,080 --> 00:16:25,600
are called in,

495
00:16:25,660 --> 00:16:26,380
that's going to result

496
00:16:26,380 --> 00:16:28,460
in continued acute inflammation.

497
00:16:29,140 --> 00:16:30,160
So although we call it

498
00:16:30,160 --> 00:16:30,720
acute inflammation

499
00:16:30,720 --> 00:16:31,840
and although it peaks

500
00:16:31,840 --> 00:16:32,480
at one day,

501
00:16:33,140 --> 00:16:33,660
acute inflammation

502
00:16:33,660 --> 00:16:35,340
is not defined by time,

503
00:16:35,340 --> 00:16:36,720
it's defined by the response.

504
00:16:37,160 --> 00:16:37,800
So for example,

505
00:16:37,900 --> 00:16:38,800
a patient could undergo

506
00:16:38,800 --> 00:16:39,360
acute inflammation

507
00:16:39,360 --> 00:16:40,460
and have acute inflammation

508
00:16:40,460 --> 00:16:41,320
for six weeks

509
00:16:41,320 --> 00:16:42,480
and that's because

510
00:16:42,480 --> 00:16:43,060
the macrophages

511
00:16:43,060 --> 00:16:44,380
keep secreting IL-8

512
00:16:44,380 --> 00:16:45,000
which calls in

513
00:16:45,000 --> 00:16:45,920
more and more neutrophils.

514
00:16:46,400 --> 00:16:47,580
So the hallmarks

515
00:16:47,580 --> 00:16:49,220
of neutrophilic inflammation,

516
00:16:49,920 --> 00:16:51,400
particularly the formation

517
00:16:51,400 --> 00:16:51,960
of pus,

518
00:16:52,320 --> 00:16:53,420
tells you that there is

519
00:16:53,420 --> 00:16:54,900
a continued acute

520
00:16:54,900 --> 00:16:56,480
inflammatory response.

521
00:16:56,920 --> 00:16:57,860
So please be aware of that

522
00:16:57,860 --> 00:16:58,820
because sometimes examiners

523
00:16:58,820 --> 00:17:00,140
like to confuse you

524
00:17:00,140 --> 00:17:01,100
by saying that there is

525
00:17:01,100 --> 00:17:02,600
a process that's been going on

526
00:17:02,600 --> 00:17:03,260
for eight weeks

527
00:17:03,260 --> 00:17:04,120
but the patient's still

528
00:17:04,120 --> 00:17:04,960
coughing up pus

529
00:17:04,960 --> 00:17:06,340
and so what is actually

530
00:17:06,340 --> 00:17:06,880
happening

531
00:17:06,880 --> 00:17:08,760
and the notion here

532
00:17:08,760 --> 00:17:09,760
is that it is still

533
00:17:09,760 --> 00:17:11,320
an example of acute inflammation

534
00:17:11,320 --> 00:17:13,020
even if it's eight weeks

535
00:17:13,020 --> 00:17:13,940
into the infection

536
00:17:13,940 --> 00:17:15,240
because of the fact

537
00:17:15,240 --> 00:17:15,960
that you still have

538
00:17:15,960 --> 00:17:17,420
a neutrophilic response

539
00:17:17,420 --> 00:17:18,120
and neutrophils

540
00:17:18,120 --> 00:17:18,940
are what define

541
00:17:18,940 --> 00:17:20,160
acute inflammation.

542
00:17:21,120 --> 00:17:22,120
Now another possibility

543
00:17:22,120 --> 00:17:23,360
is that the macrophages

544
00:17:23,360 --> 00:17:24,060
can come in

545
00:17:24,060 --> 00:17:25,180
and they could basically

546
00:17:25,180 --> 00:17:26,160
sense that

547
00:17:26,160 --> 00:17:27,040
the organism

548
00:17:27,040 --> 00:17:28,300
is a particular type

549
00:17:28,300 --> 00:17:28,720
of organism

550
00:17:28,720 --> 00:17:30,080
that needs to be walled off

551
00:17:30,080 --> 00:17:31,040
to protect the rest

552
00:17:31,040 --> 00:17:32,680
of the host,

553
00:17:32,820 --> 00:17:33,400
the organ

554
00:17:33,400 --> 00:17:34,480
and so they can

555
00:17:34,480 --> 00:17:35,360
actually result in

556
00:17:35,360 --> 00:17:36,440
they can actually

557
00:17:36,440 --> 00:17:37,120
call in

558
00:17:37,120 --> 00:17:38,660
or create an abscess

559
00:17:38,660 --> 00:17:39,480
and what's an abscess?

560
00:17:39,840 --> 00:17:40,240
An abscess

561
00:17:40,240 --> 00:17:41,640
is an area of fibrosis

562
00:17:41,640 --> 00:17:42,280
so let's pretend

563
00:17:42,280 --> 00:17:43,280
that this is an organ

564
00:17:43,280 --> 00:17:44,160
sorry

565
00:17:44,160 --> 00:17:44,980
this is an organ

566
00:17:44,980 --> 00:17:46,540
and so what the macrophages

567
00:17:46,540 --> 00:17:46,920
can do

568
00:17:46,920 --> 00:17:47,620
is they can create

569
00:17:47,620 --> 00:17:48,900
a wall of fibrosis

570
00:17:48,900 --> 00:17:49,720
around the area

571
00:17:49,720 --> 00:17:50,380
of infection

572
00:17:50,380 --> 00:17:51,980
and so that

573
00:17:51,980 --> 00:17:52,780
the inflammation

574
00:17:52,780 --> 00:17:54,020
